      "title": "Llmtee keeps usage logging off the model request path",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50451943",
      "publisher": "garritfra · Hacker News",
      "published": "2026-11-11",
      "category_key": "observability",
      "summary": "A lightweight proxy logs model usage separately from request forwarding and blocks later requests when a key exceeds its budget.",
      "body": [
        "Llmtee is a proxy that sits between an application and a language-model provider to record tokens, estimated cost, latency and time to first token. Its developer says existing logging options required extra services or changes to application code. This alternative uses a changed base URL and a local SQLite database.",
        "First, the proxy forwards a streaming response chunk by chunk while sending a copy to a usage parser. Next, it batches database writes away from the request path. According to the first-party announcement, requests continue even if SQLite is slow or unavailable. That design separates serving a response from saving its usage record; it does not establish that every record survives a storage failure.",
        "The proxy also enforces daily and monthly budgets per key. Later requests receive a provider-shaped rate-limit error after the limit is exceeded, but the request that crosses the limit still completes. Prices come from a table embedded at build time. The developer explicitly excludes evaluations, prompt management, routing, fallbacks and caching, and asks for feedback on budget semantics and future native provider support."
      ],
      "format": "full",
      "takeaway": "For teams considering Llmtee: test storage outages and budget-crossing requests before relying on its usage records or spending limits.",
