      "title": "Rystic exposes controllable API state for agent tests",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50448400",
      "publisher": "Rystic founders · Hacker News",
      "published": "2026-11-11",
      "category_key": "evals",
      "summary": "Local simulators let developers set service state, inject failures and inspect results while testing integrations and agents.",
      "body": [
        "Rystic builds local simulators of third-party APIs, including Slack, GitHub and Linear. Its founders describe them as controllable substitutes for real services when testing application code or running agent evaluations. Their starting problem is verification: generating code is fast, while shared sandboxes, account setup and manual interactions make repeatable integration checks difficult.",
        "First, a developer sets the simulator’s state. Then the test exercises the integration, with options to inject faults, add latency, use multiple actors or control rate limiting. Finally, the developer can inspect the resulting state. The announcement links a demonstration of running evaluations against the Slack simulator. These controls make a test environment adjustable; they do not prove that an agent will behave correctly against the real service.",
        "The founders say they build simulators by testing documented behavior, probing APIs and collecting traces to reconstruct state transitions. They report checking several hundred multistep workflows, running nightly checks for drift and sharing failing tests with customers. Each simulator has a fidelity card describing its match to the real API. Those are bounded compatibility claims, rather than proof of complete equivalence."
      ],
      "format": "full",
      "takeaway": "For agent evaluation teams: inspect fidelity cards and failing workflows before using a simulator as a substitute for a production integration.",
